"── 107_winui_editorkeys.mst — WG6c-2: it accepts input ──────────────────
 docs/sprints/sprint_wg6c_detail.md WG6c-2. The pane painted in WG6c-1; now
 it types.

 WHO OWNS THE CARET, and it is the one thing to get right. `handleKey:at:`
 says so itself: *the terminal owns navigation natively — mouse, arrows,
 selection — and tells the VM where the caret is only at edit time*. That was
 written for a web textarea, which owned navigation because the browser did.
 OUR pane owns it because nothing else can, so:

   * the VIEW tracks the caret for navigation (arrows, Home/End, a click),
   * the SESSION is told where it is at EDIT time and answers where it ended
     up, and the view adopts that.

 Two authorities would drift. One authority per phase cannot.

 UNDO COSTS NOTHING, which is worth saying out loud because it is the whole
 argument for the rope: `TextBuffer` is a list of ROOTS of a persistent tree,
 so the previous root IS the previous document, exactly, with no journal and
 no replay. Ctrl-Z is `buffer undo`.

 EVERY MESSAGE HERE ARRIVES AT THE DOOR because the pane is a child of our
 own window class (WG6c-1). WM_KEYDOWN and WM_CHAR were already allowlisted
 before this sprint existed; nothing was added for them.

 ── WG6c-2b: THE SELECTION, and where it lives ──────────────────────────
 The rule above already answers it. A selection is NAVIGATION — it is made
 by arrows, by Home/End and by a drag, none of which change the document —
 so the VIEW owns it, exactly as it owns the caret, and the model needs no
 selection concept at all. What the session gets told at edit time is a
 RANGE rather than a point, and `replaceFrom:length:with:` was already there
 to take it.

 ANCHOR PLUS CARET, not start plus end. The selection is the span between
 the place it started and the place the caret is now, in whichever order
 they fall — which is the only representation that survives dragging
 BACKWARDS through the anchor without the two ends swapping identity. Every
 navigation key routes through one method, `editorMoveCaretTo:`, because
 five keys agreeing about shift and a sixth not is precisely the bug this
 shape exists to make impossible.

 AND IT HAD BETTER BE VISIBLE. This project has shipped an invisible feature
 before — WG5a D4's ghost line, whose test asserted the text it WOULD use
 and never that anything appeared — so 106 paints the highlight, and the
 rectangles it paints are computed here, purely, where they can be checked
 without a window."

Object subclass: WinShell [
    <classVars: EditorCaret EditorAnchor EditorKeys EditorDragging>

    "── Routing ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> editorMessage: msg <Integer> wParam: wp <Integer> lParam: lp <Integer> ^ <Object> [
        "Every message addressed to the editor pane. Answers an LRESULT for
         what it handled and `#defwindowproc` for everything else — the same
         two-sided contract the shell's own door half keeps, so a message this
         file has no opinion about still behaves the way Windows expects.

         Captured rather than propagated: a raise in here would unwind through
         the door, and the door's own rule is that any non-Integer means
         `#defwindowproc`. Better to answer that deliberately."
        ^[ self editorMessageBody: msg wParam: wp lParam: lp ]
            on: Error do: [ :e | PaintError := e messageText. #defwindowproc ]
    ]

    WinShell class >> editorMessageBody: msg <Integer> wParam: wp <Integer> lParam: lp <Integer> ^ <Object> [
        msg = (WinApi constant: 'WM_LBUTTONDOWN') ifTrue: [ ^self editorClickAt: lp ].
        "WG6c-2b. Both already allowlisted — WG4 D5 admitted them for the
         splitter — and both reach the pane rather than the splitter's own
         handlers, because 91 routes by HWND before its handlers are consulted.
         A drag needs all three or none: a MOUSEMOVE without its LBUTTONUP
         leaves the mouse captured for the life of the window, which is the
         hazard WG4 D5 records against exactly this trio."
        msg = (WinApi constant: 'WM_MOUSEMOVE') ifTrue: [ ^self editorDragTo: lp ].
        msg = (WinApi constant: 'WM_LBUTTONUP') ifTrue: [ ^self editorEndDrag ].
        msg = (WinApi constant: 'WM_KEYDOWN') ifTrue: [ ^self editorKeyDown: wp ].
        msg = (WinApi constant: 'WM_CHAR') ifTrue: [ ^self editorChar: wp ].
        ^#defwindowproc
    ]

    "── The caret the VIEW owns ──────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> editorCaret ^ <Integer> [
        "CLAMPED ON THE WAY OUT as well as on the way in, and the difference
         matters: `editorCaret:` can only clamp against the document as it was
         when it was called, and the document changes underneath — an undo, a
         new class opened into the pane, an edit made from anywhere else. A
         caret left past the end is an `at:` away from a raise INSIDE A PAINT,
         which shows as a blank pane with nothing to say why."
        | c |
        c := EditorCaret isNil ifTrue: [ 1 ] ifFalse: [ EditorCaret ].
        ^(c max: 1) min: self editorText size + 1
    ]

    WinShell class >> editorCaretAt: n <Integer> [
        "Caret HERE, and nothing selected — the collapse `editorCaret:`
         deliberately does not do. This is what everything OUTSIDE the input
         path wants: opening a class at one of its methods, a jump from Find,
         a script or a test placing the caret before it types.

         It exists because leaving those callers to use `editorCaret:` was a
         trap, and one that had already caught two tests in this file's own
         suite: the anchor stayed where it was, so `editorCaret: 2` on a fresh
         document quietly selected the first character and the next keystroke
         replaced it."
        self editorCaret: n.
        EditorAnchor := self editorCaret.
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> resetEditorCaret [
        "A NEW DOCUMENT gets a new caret and no selection. Called from
         `openEditorOn:` in 106, which loads first and declares nothing about
         either — without this the view kept the previous class's caret, and a
         shorter document then had a selection running off its end."
        ^self editorCaretAt: 1
    ]

    WinShell class >> editorCaret: n <Integer> [
        "Clamped to the document, always. Every navigation below can run off
         an end, and a caret outside the buffer is an `at:` away from a raise
         inside a paint — which is the failure that shows as a blank pane.

         IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT TOUCH THE ANCHOR, and that is not an
         oversight to be tidied up later: shift-extend and a mouse drag both
         work by moving the caret while the anchor stays put, so a setter that
         collapsed the selection would make the two gestures this file exists
         to support impossible. The callers that DO decide are
         `editorMoveCaretTo:` (keyboard), `editorClickAt:` (mouse) and
         `adoptSessionCaret` (after an edit). Anything outside this file that
         wants a caret and no selection wants `resetEditorCaret` first."
        EditorCaret := (n max: 1) min: self editorText size + 1.
        ^self
    ]

    WinShell class >> adoptSessionCaret [
        "After an EDIT the session is the authority: it moved the caret past
         whatever it inserted or removed, and the view takes that answer
         rather than recomputing it.

         AND THE SELECTION IS GONE, which is not tidying up: the characters it
         referred to no longer exist. A selection that survived its own
         replacement would name a range in a document that has moved under it,
         and the next keystroke would delete something the user never
         highlighted."
        EditorSession current ifNotNil: [ :s | self editorCaret: s cursorOffset ].
        EditorAnchor := self editorCaret.
        ^self
    ]

    "── The selection, which the view owns for the same reason ───────────"

    WinShell class >> editorAnchor ^ <Integer> [
        "Where the selection started. Clamped on the way OUT rather than kept
         clamped, because the document can shrink under it — an undo, or an
         edit made from anywhere else — and a stale anchor must degrade to a
         valid one rather than raise inside a paint."
        | a |
        a := EditorAnchor isNil ifTrue: [ 1 ] ifFalse: [ EditorAnchor ].
        ^(a max: 1) min: self editorText size + 1
    ]

    WinShell class >> editorSelection ^ <Array> [
        "#(start length), 1-based, length 0 when there is no selection. The
         ORDER is computed here and nowhere else: anchor and caret may fall
         either way round, and every caller wants the span rather than the
         history of how it was made."
        | a c |
        a := self editorAnchor.
        c := self editorCaret.
        ^Array with: (a min: c) with: (a max: c) - (a min: c)
    ]

    WinShell class >> editorHasSelection ^ <Boolean> [
        ^(self editorSelection at: 2) > 0 ]

    WinShell class >> editorSelectedText ^ <String> [
        | sel |
        sel := self editorSelection.
        (sel at: 2) = 0 ifTrue: [ ^'' ].
        ^self editorText copyFrom: (sel at: 1) to: (sel at: 1) + (sel at: 2) - 1
    ]

    WinShell class >> editorSelectAll ^ <Object> [
        EditorAnchor := 1.
        self editorCaret: self editorText size + 1.
        self refreshEditorPane.
        self syncEditorCaret.
        ^0
    ]

    WinShell class >> shiftIsDown ^ <Boolean> [
        "GetKeyState's HIGH bit is `currently down`; the low bit is the toggle
         state, which for Shift is meaningless and for Caps Lock is the whole
         point. The same trap `controlIsDown` records, and the same answer."
        ^((WinApi getKeyState: 16r10) bitAnd: 16r8000) ~= 0   "VK_SHIFT"
    ]

    WinShell class >> editorMoveCaretTo: n <Integer> ^ <Object> [
        "EVERY navigation ends here, and this is the only place the anchor
         moves for a keyboard reason. Shift held EXTENDS — the anchor stays
         where the selection began — and shift released COLLAPSES it onto the
         caret, so there is no selection. One method rather than six, because
         five keys agreeing about shift and a sixth not is the bug that shape
         makes impossible rather than merely unlikely.

         It REPAINTS now, which plain caret movement did not have to: the
         highlight is part of the picture, so a selection that grew without a
         repaint would be a selection the user cannot see."
        self editorCaret: n.
        self shiftIsDown ifFalse: [ EditorAnchor := self editorCaret ].
        self refreshEditorPane.
        self syncEditorCaret.
        ^0
    ]

    "── Hit-testing: WG6c-1's arithmetic, inverted ───────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> editorOffsetAtX: px <Integer> y: py <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        "Pixel to 1-based offset. The forward direction is
         `column * charWidth`, `line * lineHeight` (WG6c-1), so this divides —
         which is only true because the face is MONOSPACED, and is most of why
         that was chosen.

         PURE over the text, so a click landing on the right character is
         checkable with no window. Clicking past the end of a line lands at
         its END rather than wrapping to the next, which is what every editor
         does and what a user means."
        | text targetLine targetCol i line col |
        text := self editorText.
        targetLine := py // self editorLineHeight.
        targetCol := (px + (self editorCharWidth // 2)) // self editorCharWidth.
        i := 1.
        line := 0.
        col := 0.
        [ i <= text size ] whileTrue: [
            (line = targetLine and: [ col = targetCol ]) ifTrue: [ ^i ].
            (text at: i) = Character nl
                ifTrue: [
                    "End of the target line reached before the target column:
                     the click was past the last character, so stop here."
                    line = targetLine ifTrue: [ ^i ].
                    line := line + 1.
                    col := 0 ]
                ifFalse: [ col := col + 1 ].
            i := i + 1 ].
        ^text size + 1
    ]

    WinShell class >> editorClickAt: lp <Integer> ^ <Object> [
        "A click both FOCUSES and positions. Focus first: without it the pane
         is a picture — the keys would go wherever they were going before, and
         the caret would blink in another window.

         Shift-click EXTENDS from wherever the selection already started,
         which is the one gesture that makes a long selection reachable
         without dragging across a scroll. A plain click drops the anchor
         here, so the drag below has something to be a drag FROM."
        | at |
        WinApi setFocus: self editorPaneHwnd.
        at := self editorOffsetAtX: (self loWordSigned: lp) y: (self hiWordSigned: lp).
        self editorCaret: at.
        self shiftIsDown ifFalse: [ EditorAnchor := self editorCaret ].
        "CAPTURE, so the drag keeps arriving after the pointer leaves the
         pane. Without it a selection stops growing the moment the mouse
         crosses the border, which reads as the editor losing interest."
        EditorDragging := true.
        WinApi setCapture: self editorPaneHwnd.
        self refreshEditorPane.
        self syncEditorCaret.
        ^0
    ]

    WinShell class >> editorDragTo: lp <Integer> ^ <Object> [
        "Only while a button is genuinely down. WM_MOUSEMOVE arrives for every
         pointer movement over the pane — WG4 D5 admitted it knowing that, and
         the flag is what keeps a hover from silently selecting text."
        EditorDragging == true ifFalse: [ ^#defwindowproc ].
        self editorCaret: (self editorOffsetAtX: (self loWordSigned: lp)
            y: (self hiWordSigned: lp)).
        self refreshEditorPane.
        self syncEditorCaret.
        ^0
    ]

    WinShell class >> editorEndDrag ^ <Object> [
        "RELEASED ON EVERY PATH THROUGH HERE, including one where no drag was
         in progress. A captured mouse that never sees its button-up leaves
         the pointer captured for the life of the window — WG4 D5's recorded
         hazard, and the reason the three mouse messages are admitted as a set
         rather than one at a time."
        EditorDragging := false.
        WinApi releaseCapture.
        ^0
    ]

    "── Keys ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> editorKeys ^ <Integer> [
        ^EditorKeys isNil ifTrue: [ 0 ] ifFalse: [ EditorKeys ] ]

    WinShell class >> editorKeyDown: vk <Integer> ^ <Object> [
        "Navigation and the editing keys that are not characters. Everything
         printable arrives as WM_CHAR instead and is handled there, so no key
         is acted on twice."
        | ctrl |
        ctrl := self controlIsDown.
        ctrl ifTrue: [
            vk = 16r5A ifTrue: [ ^self editorCommand: 'undo' ].   "Z"
            vk = 16r59 ifTrue: [ ^self editorCommand: 'redo' ].   "Y"
            "WG6c-2b. The clipboard verbs keep their universal bindings — an
             editor that invented its own would be wrong in a way no comment
             could fix. 108 defines them; 91 declares the no-ops."
            vk = 16r41 ifTrue: [ ^self editorSelectAll ].         "A"
            vk = 16r43 ifTrue: [ ^self editorCopy ].              "C"
            vk = 16r58 ifTrue: [ ^self editorCut ].               "X"
            vk = 16r56 ifTrue: [ ^self editorPaste ].             "V"
            ^#defwindowproc ].
        vk = 16r25 ifTrue: [ ^self editorMoveBy: -1 ].            "Left"
        vk = 16r27 ifTrue: [ ^self editorMoveBy: 1 ].             "Right"
        vk = 16r26 ifTrue: [ ^self editorMoveLine: -1 ].          "Up"
        vk = 16r28 ifTrue: [ ^self editorMoveLine: 1 ].           "Down"
        vk = 16r24 ifTrue: [ ^self editorMoveToLineEdge: #home ]. "Home"
        vk = 16r23 ifTrue: [ ^self editorMoveToLineEdge: #end ].  "End"
        vk = 16r08 ifTrue: [ ^self editorApply: 'Backspace' ].    "Backspace"
        vk = 16r2E ifTrue: [ ^self editorApply: 'Delete' ].       "Delete"
        vk = 16r0D ifTrue: [ ^self editorApply: 'Enter' ].        "Enter"
        ^#defwindowproc
    ]

    WinShell class >> editorChar: code <Integer> ^ <Object> [
        "Printable characters only. Backspace (8), Enter (13) and Tab (9) all
         arrive here as well as in WM_KEYDOWN, and acting on both would insert
         twice or delete twice — so the control range is declined here and
         owned there. Tab is the exception worth naming: it is not handled at
         all yet, so it falls through to the system rather than inserting an
         invisible character nobody asked for."
        code < 32 ifTrue: [ ^#defwindowproc ].
        code = 127 ifTrue: [ ^#defwindowproc ].
        ^self editorApply: (String with: (Character value: code))
    ]

    WinShell class >> editorApply: key <String> ^ <Object> [
        "One edit. The view hands over its caret — or its SELECTION, when
         there is one — the session applies the edit and moves the caret, and
         the view adopts the answer. The one-authority-per-phase rule this
         file exists to keep, with a range in place of a point.

         A selection makes every editing key mean the same thing: replace what
         is highlighted. Typing replaces it with the character, Enter with a
         newline, and Backspace or Delete with nothing at all — which is why
         they need no special case beyond the empty string."
        | sel |
        EditorSession current isNil ifTrue: [ ^#defwindowproc ].
        EditorKeys := self editorKeys + 1.
        sel := self editorSelection.
        (sel at: 2) > 0
            ifTrue: [
                EditorSession current
                    replaceFrom: (sel at: 1) - 1
                    length: (sel at: 2)
                    with: (self editorReplacementFor: key) ]
            ifFalse: [ EditorSession current handleKey: key at: self editorCaret ].
        self adoptSessionCaret.
        self refreshEditorPane.
        self syncEditorCaret.
        ^0
    ]

    WinShell class >> editorReplacementFor: key <String> ^ <String> [
        "What a key puts in place of a selection. Named separately so the
         three editing keys that are not characters cannot drift from the one
         that is."
        key = 'Enter' ifTrue: [ ^String with: Character nl ].
        key = 'Backspace' ifTrue: [ ^'' ].
        key = 'Delete' ifTrue: [ ^'' ].
        ^key
    ]

    WinShell class >> editorInsertText: s <String> ^ <Object> [
        "LITERAL TEXT, never a key name — and that distinction is the whole
         reason this is not just `editorApply:`. The key vocabulary has three
         words in it ('Enter', 'Backspace', 'Delete'), so pasting a snippet
         that happened to be the word `Delete` would delete the selection
         instead of inserting the word. Unlikely, silent, and impossible to
         work out from the symptom.

         One path for both cases: with no selection the range is empty and
         `replaceFrom:length:with:` is a plain insertion at the caret, so
         paste needs no branch and cannot get one of the two wrong."
        | sel |
        EditorSession current isNil ifTrue: [ ^#defwindowproc ].
        EditorKeys := self editorKeys + 1.
        sel := self editorSelection.
        EditorSession current
            replaceFrom: (sel at: 1) - 1 length: (sel at: 2) with: s.
        self adoptSessionCaret.
        self refreshEditorPane.
        self syncEditorCaret.
        ^0
    ]

    WinShell class >> editorCommand: name <String> ^ <Object> [
        "Undo and redo, which the rope makes free: `TextBuffer` keeps the
         ROOTS of a persistent tree, so the previous root is the previous
         document exactly. No journal, no replay, no partial application."
        EditorSession current isNil ifTrue: [ ^#defwindowproc ].
        EditorSession current handleCommand: name.
        self adoptSessionCaret.
        self refreshEditorPane.
        self syncEditorCaret.
        ^0
    ]

    "── Navigation, all pure arithmetic over the text ────────────────────"

    WinShell class >> editorMoveBy: delta <Integer> ^ <Object> [
        ^self editorMoveCaretTo: self editorCaret + delta
    ]

    WinShell class >> editorLineColOf: offset <Integer> ^ <Array> [
        "#(line col), both 0-based. Shared by every vertical move and by the
         caret's own pixel position, so they cannot disagree about where the
         caret is."
        | text i line col |
        text := self editorText.
        i := 1. line := 0. col := 0.
        [ i < offset and: [ i <= text size ] ] whileTrue: [
            (text at: i) = Character nl
                ifTrue: [ line := line + 1. col := 0 ]
                ifFalse: [ col := col + 1 ].
            i := i + 1 ].
        ^Array with: line with: col
    ]

    WinShell class >> editorOffsetOfLine: targetLine <Integer> col: targetCol <Integer> ^ <Integer> [
        | text i line col |
        text := self editorText.
        i := 1. line := 0. col := 0.
        [ i <= text size ] whileTrue: [
            (line = targetLine and: [ col = targetCol ]) ifTrue: [ ^i ].
            (text at: i) = Character nl
                ifTrue: [
                    line = targetLine ifTrue: [ ^i ].
                    line := line + 1. col := 0 ]
                ifFalse: [ col := col + 1 ].
            i := i + 1 ].
        ^text size + 1
    ]

    WinShell class >> editorMoveLine: delta <Integer> ^ <Object> [
        "Up and down keep the COLUMN, which is what makes vertical movement
         feel like movement rather than teleporting. A short line clamps to
         its end and the column is not remembered past that — the simple
         behaviour, and the one that never surprises."
        | lc |
        lc := self editorLineColOf: self editorCaret.
        ^self editorMoveCaretTo: (self editorOffsetOfLine: ((lc at: 1) + delta max: 0)
            col: (lc at: 2))
    ]

    WinShell class >> editorMoveToLineEdge: which <Symbol> ^ <Object> [
        | lc |
        lc := self editorLineColOf: self editorCaret.
        ^self editorMoveCaretTo: (which == #home
            ifTrue: [ self editorOffsetOfLine: (lc at: 1) col: 0 ]
            ifFalse: [ self editorOffsetOfLine: (lc at: 1) col: 1000000 ])
    ]

    "── The selection's rectangles, PURE so they can be checked ──────────

     One rect per line the selection touches, in the pane's own coordinates,
     which 106 fills before it draws the text. Computed here rather than
     inside the paint for the reason this whole port keeps returning to: a
     calculation in a paint handler is a calculation nobody can check, because
     a raise in there is captured and the pane simply stays blank."

    WinShell class >> editorSelectionRectsAtX: ox <Integer> y: oy <Integer> ^ <OrderedCollection> [
        | out sel text lo hi charW lineH i line col startCol lineStart |
        out := OrderedCollection new.
        sel := self editorSelection.
        (sel at: 2) = 0 ifTrue: [ ^out ].
        text := self editorText.
        lo := sel at: 1.
        hi := lo + (sel at: 2).
        charW := self editorCharWidth.
        lineH := self editorLineHeight.
        i := 1. line := 0. col := 0.
        startCol := nil.
        lineStart := nil.
        [ i < hi ] whileTrue: [
            (i >= lo and: [ startCol isNil ]) ifTrue: [ startCol := col. lineStart := line ].
            (i <= text size and: [ (text at: i) = Character nl ])
                ifTrue: [
                    "A newline INSIDE the selection ends this line's rect. The
                     +1 column is the newline itself, drawn as a sliver so a
                     selected line break is visible rather than implied."
                    startCol isNil ifFalse: [
                        out add: (Array
                            with: ox + (startCol * charW)
                            with: oy + (lineStart * lineH)
                            with: ((col - startCol + 1) * charW)
                            with: lineH).
                        startCol := nil ].
                    line := line + 1. col := 0 ]
                ifFalse: [ col := col + 1 ].
            i := i + 1 ].
        startCol isNil ifFalse: [
            out add: (Array
                with: ox + (startCol * charW)
                with: oy + (lineStart * lineH)
                with: ((col - startCol) * charW)
                with: lineH) ].
        ^out
    ]
]
